WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.830
Few believed that the frail, asthmatic little
boy who loved to collect insects would amount

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:12.040
to anything special, much less the youngest
and most robust president in US history.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:17.240
Theodore Roosevelt’s transformation from
sickly child to master of the bully pulpit

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:23.029
and the ultimate symbol of American strength
and vitality is an inspiring story of the

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:25.419
power of self determination.

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:34.000
In this week’s Biographics we uncover his
story.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.540
Early Life

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:45.090
Theodore Roosevelt, the future 26th President
of the United States, was born in New York

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:52.060
City on October, 27th, 1858 to Theodore Roosevelt
Senior and Martha Bulloch Roosevelt.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.210
Roosevelt, senior was one of the leading citizens
of New York City.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:01.190
Theodore Junior, who was known as Teedie was
the second child to the affluent couple, who

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.960
lived in a luxurious home on Manhattan Island.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.770
Teedie had a sister, Anna, who was four years
older than him.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:11.530
A younger brother, Elliot, would follow two
years later, and then another sister, Corrine,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.390
a year after that.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:16.610
Teedie suffered from asthma from the age of
three.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.120
His condition was so bad that he spent much
of his early years in bed.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.400
Unable to attend school, his parents hired
tutors to teach him from home.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.590
Despite his illness, young Teedie relished
the opportunity to learn.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.450
Once he had learned to read, he became a voracious
devourer of books.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:38.480
He loved to fill his mind with tales of such
Nordic heroes as King Olaf and Eric the Red.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.910
He was also fascinated with newspaper stories
about the exploits of the famous British explorer

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.380
Dr. David Livingstone.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.470
Teedie was a dreamer and he filled his mind
with dreams of tracking through uncharted

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:54.290
territories and discovering untamed tribes.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.990
Young Teedie showed an unusual curiosity about
the natural world.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:02.900
One day, on a trip to the market, he came
across a dead seal in a butcher’s stall.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:08.429
He was fascinated with the find and returned
daily to study and measure it with his ruler.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.379
Eventually he managed to take possession of
the seal’s skeleton and take it home where

00:02:12.379 --> 00:02:16.840
it became the prize exhibit of his ‘Roosevelt
Museum of Natural History’, which he had

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.769
set up in his bedroom.

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:23.620
From the age of six, Teedie began filling
journals with his observations about the natural

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.620
world.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.959
His mother regularly took him on trips to
the country in order to help alleviate his

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:28.959
asthma.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:33.629
Teedie made the most of the opportunity to
wander around in search of fish, insects,

00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:37.209
eels and birds that he could analyze and write
about.

00:02:37.209 --> 00:02:41.519
The Roosevelt children were raised under the
norms of Victorian society.

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:45.909
As members of the upper class they did not
mix with the less wealthy, strict mores of

00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:51.109
decency were enforced and manners and politeness
were of utmost importance.

00:02:51.109 --> 00:02:56.709
Sunday was the day for religious observance,
consisting of church followed by quiet contemplation.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:03:01.799
Teedie hated Sundays as this was the day when
he was not permitted to indulge his natural

00:03:01.799 --> 00:03:03.370
curiosity of the world.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:08.529
In 1869, when he was ten years of age, Teedie’s
family embarked on a tour of Europe.

00:03:08.529 --> 00:03:13.379
For twelve months the children were exposed
to the great sights of England, Germany, France

00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:14.379
and Italy.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:16.340
Yet, Teedie was not impressed.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.310
He later wrote . . .
I cordially hated it, as did my younger brother

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.310
and sister.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:26.049
Practically all the enjoyment we had was in
exploring any ruins or mountains when we get

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:30.310
away from our elders, and in playing in our
different hotels.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.849
When he was thirteen, Teedie’s father gave
him his first shotgun.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:39.079
He was dismayed to find that he was a terrible
shot, which brought ridicule from his peers.

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:42.870
When a friend challenged him to hit a billboard
in the distance, he discovered what the problem

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.999
was, later recalling . . .
Not only was I unable to read the sign, but

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:48.200
I could not even see the letters.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.409
He was extremely near-sighted, but had never
realized it.

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:55.409
After telling his father about the problem,
he was given a pair of eyeglasses.

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:59.900
He wrote in his diary . . .
I had no idea how beautiful the world was

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.939
until I got those spectacles.

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:06.879
With his improved eyesight, Teedie was able
to become even more obsessive with his nature

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:07.879
studies.

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:11.930
He learned taxidermy, stuffing and mounting
birds that he shot and adding them to his

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.170
ever-expanding natural history museum.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:19.299
In the winter of 1870-71, the Roosevelt’s
made a trip to the Mediterranean, visiting

00:04:19.299 --> 00:04:22.389
Egypt, the Holy Land, Syria, Greece and Turkey.

00:04:22.389 --> 00:04:25.280
Unlike the trip to Europe, Teedie loved this
experience.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.020
He made the most of the opportunity to shoot
exotic birds and stuff them to take home and

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.370
add to his collection.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:36.070
When he was 13, Teedie was sent off to Maine
for a solo trip to improve his health.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.300
On the train journey north, he found himself
in a cabin with some other teenage boys.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:44.500
In the skinny, weak, bespectacled asthmatic
they found an easy mark.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.349
They roughed him up, leaving him bruised and
humiliated.

00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:49.750
When he returned home, he told his father
about the incident.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.500
Roosevelt senior told his son that he needed
to develop his body physically so that he

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.620
could defend himself.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.069
A prizefighter was hired to teach the boy
how to box.

00:04:59.069 --> 00:05:02.800
From this point onward, Teedie became obsessed
with building his body.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.460
He began lifting weights, doing calisthenics
and running.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.870
He developed a great admiration for those
who possessed strength and displayed manly

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:10.870
virility.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:15.600
And he was determined to live what he called
‘the strenuous life’.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:22.389
A Harvard Man
The Roosevelt’s were a family that were

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:26.229
driven by goals and, after returning from
their trip to the Middle East, they set one

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:31.360
for their oldest son; to gain entry to Harvard
College in three years.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.780
This was a terrific challenge for young Teedie.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.490
He had never gone to school and, although
he was well skilled in languages, nature and

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.690
the classics, he was deficient in mathematics.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:44.312
A special tutor was hired and, over the next
two years, he worked hard to improve his skills.

00:05:44.312 --> 00:05:50.470
The hard work paid off and in 1875 he passed
the Harvard entrance exams.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:55.169
For a freshman at Harvard, the childhood name
of Teedie would never do, so he now took to

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:56.970
calling himself Theodore.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:01.759
He immediately took to college life, involving
himself in political parades, boxing tournaments

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:02.930
and rowing competition.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:08.030
He also became a skilled debater, taking on
professors and fellow students alike.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:13.229
To his delight, he found himself readily accepted
and was soon invited to join some of Harvard’s

00:06:13.229 --> 00:06:14.969
most prestigious societies.

00:06:14.969 --> 00:06:20.140
Theodore’s blissful existence was interrupted
during a visit home during his Sophomore year.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.840
He found that his father was gravely ill.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:27.040
With his beloved Teedie back home, the old
man’s health revived somewhat and the danger

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.470
seemed to have passed.

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:32.330
But when Theodore returned to Harvard for
the new year, his father took a sharp turn

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.330
for the worse.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:37.099
Towards the end of February, Theodore received
a telegram to return home urgently.

00:06:37.099 --> 00:06:40.819
He never made it in time, with his father
dying that night.

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:44.150
Theodore was deeply depressed as a result
of his father’s death.

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:47.770
However, he refused to show his pain to others.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:51.379
Instigating a pattern that he would continue
for the rest of his life, he soldiered on,

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:54.449
disciplining his mind and body to do what
had to be done.

00:06:54.449 --> 00:06:57.550
Still his grief became evident in his actions.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.319
During a Summer family vacation to Oyster
Bay he rode his horse to exhaustion.

00:07:01.319 --> 00:07:05.490
When a dog insisted on chasing the horse he
turned and shot it.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.520
Theodore also began hiking, finding his release
in the great outdoors.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.860
He teamed with a crusty old adventurer by
the name of Bill Sewell, who served as his

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:14.860
guide.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:18.360
Sewell later recorded his impression of Theodore
at the time . . .

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.599
He was fair minded, Theodore was.

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:23.120
And then he took pains to learn everything.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:25.009
There was nothing beneath his notice.

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:26.539
I liked him right off.

00:07:26.539 --> 00:07:27.749
I liked him clear through.

00:07:27.749 --> 00:07:30.050
There wasn’t a quality in him I didn’t
like.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:34.319
He wasn’t headlong or aggressive, except
when necessary, and as far as I could see

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:38.060
he wasn’t a bit cocky, though other folks
thought so.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.280
By his mid-teens, Theodore had developed into
quite a character.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.879
Never afraid to express his opinion, he could
always be counted on to be the center of attention.

00:07:45.879 --> 00:07:51.249
His unique outbursts of approval, ‘Bully!’
and ‘De-lighted!’ quickly became trademark

00:07:51.249 --> 00:07:52.249
responses.

00:07:52.249 --> 00:07:56.110
In 1879 he set his charms on a young woman
by the name of Alice Lee.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:01.139
‘See that girl, he said to a Harvard pal,
‘she won’t have me, but I m going to have

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:02.319
her’.

00:08:02.319 --> 00:08:03.800
And have her he did.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.520
He set out on a campaign to win Alice’s
heart, leading to their engagement in early

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.520
1880.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.199
Theodore graduated from Harvard in June of
that year, 21st in his class of 160 students.

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:17.879
Then, in October, he and Alice were married.

00:08:17.879 --> 00:08:21.720
After a short honeymoon, Theodore enrolled
in a law degree at Columbia University, while

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.009
also working at his uncle’s law firm.

00:08:24.009 --> 00:08:27.939
During his limited leisure time he decided
to learn about the War of 1812.

00:08:27.939 --> 00:08:32.930
When he couldn’t find a decent book on the
subject, he decided to write one himself.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.110
During the Summer of 1881, the young Roosevelt
couple took a trip to Europe.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:40.580
While in Switzerland, Theodore left his wife
behind while he climbed the Matterhorn.

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:44.370
It was during this trip that he decided to
forego a legal career.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:50.500
He was determined to set his sights on becoming
a politician.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:57.530
Entering the Arena
Back then, politics was seen as being beneath

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:02.220
men of wealth and friends and family tried
to dissuade Theodore’s ambitions.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:06.890
But that was always going to be a losing proposition
and, on returning to New York, he joined the

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:07.910
Republican Party.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:12.520
Here he was associating with a class of men
that he hadn’t previously mixed with – rough

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:17.130
men, who were skilled in the low blows and
dirty tricks of state politics.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.850
Roosevelt soon learned their ways and, within
months had won the party’s nomination for

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.340
a seat in the New York State Assembly.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.370
It was a solid Republican seat and Roosevelt
won it handily.

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:31.660
Roosevelt turned up to take his seat at the
New York state capital in Albany on January

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.500
22nd, 1882.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.750
He cut the figure of a dandy with his fine
suit and upper crust ways.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:41.150
He was ridiculed by Democrats and Republicans
alike.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:46.890
Despite this he, according to his own recollection,
‘rose like a rocket’ in New York politics.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:50.210
Roosevelt’s politics were grounded in self-determination.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:54.290
He firmly believed that each man was in control
of his own destiny.

00:09:54.290 --> 00:10:00.450
But he also realized that the system was grossly
unfair, with those with the money and power

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.130
having all the opportunities.

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:08.000
He made it his goal to bring about a fairer
system where all Americans had the opportunity

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.150
to succeed.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:13.680
When he wasn’t working his way up the political
ladder in New York, Theodore ventured out

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.710
west in order to fulfil his love of adventure
and the outdoors.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:23.460
In September 1883, he made an excursion to
the Dakota Badlands, with the express purpose

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.410
of bringing home a buffalo head as a trophy.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:30.350
As with most things he set his mind to, he
was successful in that goal.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:33.050
During this trip, he also purchased a cattle
ranch.

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:37.340
In November 1883, Theodore won his 3rd term
to the New York State Assembly.

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:41.690
He returned to Albany while his wife, who
was heavily pregnant with their first child

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.480
went to live with his sister.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:49.680
On February 11, 1884 he received a long-awaited
telegram announcing the birth of a baby girl.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.060
But it also told him that Alice was very sick.

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:58.550
He rushed home to discover that the news was
even worse – both his wife and his mother

00:10:58.550 --> 00:10:59.710
were gravely ill.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:05.110
They both died on the same day, February 14th,
1884.

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:08.990
That night he wrote in his diary . . .
The light has gone from my life.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:14.380
Theodore responded to the double tragedy with
the stoicism that epitomized his personality.

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:18.480
After a few days of isolation, he left the
baby in the care of his sister and returned

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:21.630
to Albany to see out his term in the General
Assembly.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.920
Life had to go on.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.290
Throughout 1884, Roosevelt worked tirelessly
to propel himself from state to national politics.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:33.090
His brash, unwavering style brought him to
the attention of prominent men, including

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:37.670
an influential Bostonian by the name of Henry
Cabot Lodge.

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:42.490
By the end of the year the 25-year-old, was
being eyed by many as a future Republican

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:43.490
leader.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:46.030
However, inside Roosevelt was an empty man.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:51.070
He needed to get away from the rough and tumble
of politics in order to heal himself.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:56.640
At the end of 1884, Roosevelt retired from
politics and moved to one of his ranches in

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.380
North Dakota.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:05.540
Cowboy
Theodore Roosevelt turned up in the small

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:10.930
Dakota town of Medora dressed in buckskins,
brandishing a brand new Winchester rifle and

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.550
with a hunting knife in his belt that he’d
bought from Tiffany’s Jewellers.

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:18.220
He was the quintessential ‘dude’ and was
soon ridiculed by the hardened locals.

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:21.240
Despite this, he reveled in life in the west.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.740
He displayed a steely courage which impressed
all who came to know him.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.150
Roosevelt hired his old tramping guide Bill
Sewell to come out and mange his ranch for

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.150
him.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.150
But he didn’t leave all the hard wok to
his men.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:38.350
He rode out on the range with them, chased
spooked cattle and stood guard during long,

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:39.730
bitterly cold nights.

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:45.020
In 1886, he captured a group of thieves who
had stolen a boat and then took them his prisoners

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:47.650
on a three-day trek to the local sheriff.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:52.880
During those 3 days he didn’t sleep a wink
as he kept his rifle trained on his captives.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:58.940
Following the exceptional harsh winter of
1886-87, which killed half of his cattle stock,

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.960
Roosevelt returned east.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:05.490
He was thirty pounds heavier than we he left
and was a harder, more confident version of

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.510
himself.

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:11.170
Soon after returning he met up with and fell
in love with a childhood friend maned Edith

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:12.170
Carrow.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:18.260
They were married on December 2nd, 1886 and
would go on to have five children together.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:24.780
Political Ascendancy
Roosevelt was soon approached by Republican

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:27.070
leaders and urged to re-enter politics.

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:29.670
They wanted him to run for mayor of New York.

00:13:29.670 --> 00:13:33.530
He accepted and campaigned hard, despite having
little chance of winning.

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.990
He came in third place with 27 percent of
the vote.

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:41.620
Thinking that his political career was over,
he next put his efforts into the writing of

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.720
his book, The Winning of the West.

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:48.290
In 1888, Roosevelt gave stump speeches for
Republican Presidential candidate Benjamin

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:49.290
Harrison.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:53.690
When he won the Presidency, Harrison was persuaded
by Theodore’s political champion Henry Cabot

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:58.110
Lodge, to appoint the energetic Roosevelt
to the US Civil Service Commission.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:03.510
He took to the role with a vengeance, determined
to fight the corrupt spoils system and enforce

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:04.840
civil service laws.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:09.740
In 1894, Roosevelt was appointed President
of the Board of New York City Police Commissioners.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:13.780
He then set about making improvements to the
structure of the police force, rooting out

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:17.910
corruption and imposing more stringent standards
for recruitment.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:22.880
In 1897, again under the urging of Henry Cabot
Lodge, new President William McKinley appointed

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.600
Roosevelt as the assistant secretary of the
Navy.

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:29.600
He became a strong advocate for the building
of the strength of the navy.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:35.490
When on February 15th, 1898 the US warship
Maine exploded in Havana harbor, Roosevelt

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:39.570
took it upon himself to order a number of
ships to prepare for war.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.640
When war was declared a short time later,
Roosevelt resigned his post and formed the

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.980
First US Volunteer Cavalry Regiment.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:50.100
He was determined to be in the thick of the
action.

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:57.670
The Rough Riders
The press dubbed Roosevelt’s cavalry regiment

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:58.940
the ‘Rough Riders’.

00:14:58.940 --> 00:15:03.060
After a period of training in San Antonio,
Texas, they were shipped to Cuba.

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:07.690
Their first action was at the Battle of Las
Guasimas, where they forced the Spanish to

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.270
abandon their entrenched positions.

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:14.840
On July 1, 1898, Roosevelt led his men on
the famous charge up Kettle Hill.

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:18.060
He was the only man on horseback, making an
easy target for the enemy.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:22.230
Still, without any care for his own safety,
he rode up and down the line, urging his men

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:23.230
up the hill.

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:27.000
Also attempting to take the hill were soldiers
of the Ninth US Cavalry, but these soldiers

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.690
were having a hard time of it.

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:30.980
A reporter for the New York Times recorded
the action . . .

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:35.790
Colonel Roosevelt, on horseback, broke from
the woods behind the line of the Ninth, and

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:40.360
finding its men lying in his way, shouted:
“If you don’t wish to go forward, let

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.110
my men pass, please.”

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:45.910
The junior officers of the Ninth, with their
Negroes, instantly sprang into line with the

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:49.640
Rough Riders, and charged at the blue block-house
on the right.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.270
I speak of Roosevelt first because... he was,
without doubt, the most conspicuous figure

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:58.930
in the charge.... Roosevelt, mounted high
on horseback, and charging the rifle-pits

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:02.790
at a gallop and quite alone, made you feel
that you would like to cheer.

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:07.670
He wore on his sombrero a blue polka-dot handkerchief...
which, as he advanced, floated out straight

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:09.730
behind his head, like a guidon.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:13.560
Having reached the crest of the hill, Roosevelt
dismounted and urged his men to follow him.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:17.170
But there was yet another hill to conquer
and no sooner had the Rough Riders scrambled

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:21.350
to the top of Kettle Hill than their dashing
colonel set off for San Juan Hill.

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:25.230
His men overpowered the Spanish resistance
and took possession of both hills.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:29.510
In the process, Roosevelt killed a Spanish
trooper who, he said, ‘doubled over like

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:30.900
a jack-rabbit’.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:34.960
Following victory in Cuba, Roosevelt returned
to New York, where he was convinced to run

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:35.960
for Governor.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:40.920
Campaigning vigorously on his war record he
won by just 1 percent of the popular vote.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:44.880
During his time as governor, he developed
the style and platform which would underpin

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:45.880
his presidency.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:50.400
He was a passionate advocate for the rights
of the underprivileged, was against large

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:55.850
corporations and monopolies and championed
the preservation of national resources.

00:16:55.850 --> 00:17:00.770
In 1900, Henry Cabot Lodge and other prominent
Republicans urged Roosevelt to throw his hat

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:06.209
into the ring for the vacant Republican Vice-Presidential
spot in the upcoming Presidential elections.

00:17:06.209 --> 00:17:10.939
Though reluctant, he won the Republican nomination,
pairing with President McKinley.

00:17:10.939 --> 00:17:17.540
With his boundless energy he threw himself
into a barnstorming campaign, making 480 speeches

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:18.750
in 23 states.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:24.040
In the end, he and McKinley easily won and
Theodore Roosevelt was sworn in as Vice President

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:27.630
of the United States on March 1st, 1901.

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:31.610
Six months later, President McKinley was shot
while attending the Pan-American exposition

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:32.770
in New York.

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.500
He died eight days later.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:38.840
As a result, the 42-year-old Roosevelt was
propelled into the highest office in the land,

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:43.680
becoming the 26th President of the United
States on September 14, 1901.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:50.610
He was the youngest man to ever hold the position.

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:55.130
POTUS
Roosevelt was a very different man to William

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:56.130
McKinley.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.910
Still, he vowed that he would continue to
pursue the policies of his predecessor.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:04.770
At the same time, he was determined to stamp
his mark on the Presidency in order to win

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:06.850
a second term in his own right.

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:10.950
A large part of the focus of Roosevelt’s
domestic policies were focused on breaking

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.100
up the monopolies of big business.

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:17.930
He instigated 44 anti-trust suits, forcing
the breaking up of the Northern Securities

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:21.240
Company and putting regulatory controls on
Standard Oil.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:25.750
Of all the domestic policies that Roosevelt
was passionate about, however, none came close

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:29.080
to the preservation of wildlife and the environment.

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:33.690
He established the United States Forest Service,
created five national parks and placed a total

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:37.490
of 230 million acres of land under public
protection.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:41.070
In terms of foreign policy, Roosevelt was
an ardent imperialist.

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:44.900
He successfully mediated the Treaty of Portsmouth
which brought an end to the Russo-Japanese

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.800
War, winning a Nobel Peace Prize for his efforts.

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:52.290
His making of a deal with the Panamanian government
for the building of a canal led to the separation

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.530
of Panama from Columbia in 1903.

00:18:54.530 --> 00:19:01.500
In the 1904 Presidential election, the Democrats
put up Alton Brooks Parker to face Roosevelt.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:05.980
Campaigning on the promise to give every American
a ‘square deal’, Roosevelt won 56% of

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:12.060
the popular vote, along with 336 of 476 electoral
votes.

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:15.641
During his second term in office, Roosevelt
began moving to the left of his Republican

00:19:15.641 --> 00:19:16.641
base.

00:19:16.641 --> 00:19:20.670
He pushed for a number of quite radical reforms,
including the 8-hour work day, most of which

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:22.090
were defeated in the House.

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:27.120
He had declared that he would not seek a third
term and so became a lame duck President.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:31.420
While thoroughly enjoying the role of President
he was felt that staying in office for more

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:35.720
than two terms would run the risk of dictatorship.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:43.360
Post Presidency
Following the end of his second term in office

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:47.180
in March, 1909, Roosevelt went on an African
safari with a group of prominent scientists

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:48.180
and hunters.

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:52.630
The expedition was on behalf of the Smithsonian
Institute and was charged with bringing back

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:54.960
specimens for the Institute’s museum.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:20:00.500
The trip was successful, and the group came
back with 11,400 specimens, ranging from insects

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:01.590
to elephants.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:06.000
When he returned to the United States, Roosevelt
was far from happy with the performance of

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:11.270
his successor, William Taft, believing that
Taft had made the Republicans far too conservative.

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:17.190
He began to speak loudly of leading a program
of New Nationalism, which emphasized labor

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:18.190
over capital.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:23.300
By the time that the 1912 Presidential election
rolled around, Roosevelt was convinced that

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:26.130
only he could save the Republican Party.

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:28.330
He challenged Taft in the Republican Primary.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:31.990
However, Taft won the nomination, squeezing
Roosevelt to the sidelines.

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:36.920
Undeterred, Roosevelt quit the Republicans,
formed his own party, the Progressives, and

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:38.830
ran for president as an independent.

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.610
He then went to the stumps and appealed to
the people.

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:46.360
On October 14, 1912 Roosevelt was giving a
speech in Milwaukee, Wisconsin when he was

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:48.730
struck by an assassin’s bullet.

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:54.740
It was a perfect shot to the heart, penetrating
his breast pocket and slamming into his thick

00:20:54.740 --> 00:20:55.760
glasses case.

00:20:55.760 --> 00:21:00.590
The bullet then thumped into a heavy speech
manuscript that he had chosen not to use.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:05.040
Without missing a beat, Roosevelt, ensured
that the would-be killer was apprehended than

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.490
when right on with his speech.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:08.830
His first words to the crowd after being hit
were . . .

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:12.730
Ladies and gentlemen I don’t know whether
you fully understand that I have just been

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:16.570
shot but it takes more than that to kill a
Bull Moose.

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:20.240
The bullet stayed in his pectoral muscle until
his death.

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:24.901
The 1912 Presidential election was won by
Democratic candidate Woodrow Wilson, with

00:21:24.901 --> 00:21:27.080
42 percent of the popular vote.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:32.940
However, Roosevelt did beat the incumbent
president with 27 percent compared to 23 percent

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:33.990
for Taft.

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:39.490
During a South American Expedition in 1913-14,
Roosevelt contracted tropical fever after

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:42.450
suffering a leg wound, which became badly
infected.

00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:47.180
By the time he was returned to New York he
was 50 pounds lighter and deathly pale.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:55.920
The Old Lion is Dead
The last few years were racked with pain for

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:58.220
the once robust outdoor adventurer.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:01.950
Malaria bouts were common as were complications
of his leg inflammation.

00:22:01.950 --> 00:22:06.540
The end came in the very early morning hours
of January 6th 1919, after he had complained

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:08.720
about not being able to breathe the night
before.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:12.310
A doctor was summoned and, after some treatment,
he felt better.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:16.290
However, he died in his sleep as a result
of a blood clot in the lungs.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:20.860
When he heard of his father’s demise, oldest
son Archibald telegraphed his siblings with

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:24.100
the words . . .
The old lion is dead.

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:54.270
Theodore Roosevelt was 60 years old.

